WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.921
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C two, Book two.

2
00:00:05.221 --> 00:00:08.561
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.861 --> 00:00:15.008
Unit fifteen: Decision Theory. Today you will compare choices using rather than + gerund.

4
00:00:15.308 --> 00:00:18.122
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.067 --> 00:00:22.369
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.217 --> 00:00:24.520
Decision theory.

7
00:00:25.517 --> 00:00:29.525
Decision theory means the study of how choices are or should be made.

8
00:00:30.117 --> 00:00:33.869
Decision theory links mathematics, economics and psychology.

9
00:00:34.483 --> 00:00:37.147
Your turn. Say: Decision theory.

10
00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:41.373
Decision theory.

11
00:00:42.117 --> 00:00:43.663
Expected utility.

12
00:00:44.650 --> 00:00:49.355
Expected utility means the average value of an outcome weighted by its probability.

13
00:00:49.950 --> 00:00:52.936
She calculated the expected utility of each option.

14
00:00:53.550 --> 00:00:56.527
Your turn. Say: Expected utility.

15
00:00:59.367 --> 00:01:00.948
Expected utility.

16
00:01:01.683 --> 00:01:02.893
Risk aversion.

17
00:01:03.883 --> 00:01:08.472
Risk aversion means a preference for a certain outcome over a gamble of equal value.

18
00:01:09.083 --> 00:01:12.255
Risk aversion explains why people buy insurance.

19
00:01:12.850 --> 00:01:16.315
Your turn. Say: Risk aversion.

20
00:01:19.167 --> 00:01:20.365
Risk aversion.

21
00:01:21.117 --> 00:01:22.536
Bounded rationality.

22
00:01:23.550 --> 00:01:29.834
Bounded rationality means the idea that reasoning is limited by time, information and mental capacity.

23
00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:34.017
Herbert Simon introduced bounded rationality to economics.

24
00:01:34.617 --> 00:01:37.373
Your turn. Say: Bounded rationality.

25
00:01:40.233 --> 00:01:41.838
Bounded rationality.

26
00:01:42.583 --> 00:01:43.596
Heuristic.

27
00:01:44.583 --> 00:01:48.394
Heuristic means a mental shortcut that gives a quick, workable judgment.

28
00:01:48.983 --> 00:01:52.202
A heuristic such as 'choose the familiar brand' saves effort.

29
00:01:52.817 --> 00:01:55.469
Your turn. Say: Heuristic.

30
00:01:58.333 --> 00:01:59.323
Heuristic.

31
00:02:00.083 --> 00:02:01.154
Uncertainty.

32
00:02:02.150 --> 00:02:06.994
Uncertainty means a situation in which outcomes or probabilities are unknown.

33
00:02:07.583 --> 00:02:10.918
Uncertainty differs from risk, where probabilities are known.

34
00:02:11.517 --> 00:02:14.053
Your turn. Say: Uncertainty.

35
00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:17.948
Uncertainty.

36
00:02:18.683 --> 00:02:19.824
Trade-off.

37
00:02:20.817 --> 00:02:25.254
Trade-off means a balance in which gaining one thing means losing another.

38
00:02:25.850 --> 00:02:29.939
Every design involves a trade-off between cost and quality.

39
00:02:30.550 --> 00:02:33.446
Your turn. Say: Trade-off.

40
00:02:36.300 --> 00:02:37.847
Trade-off.

41
00:02:38.583 --> 00:02:40.037
Optimal choice.

42
00:02:41.050 --> 00:02:44.919
Optimal choice means the best available option given one's goals.

43
00:02:45.517 --> 00:02:48.782
The optimal choice is rarely obvious in advance.

44
00:02:49.383 --> 00:02:52.535
Your turn. Say: Optimal choice.

45
00:02:55.400 --> 00:02:56.900
Optimal choice.

46
00:02:57.700 --> 00:03:01.188
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:03:02.050 --> 00:03:07.466
Visal, I spent two hours comparing laptops and still felt unsure. What went wrong?

48
00:03:08.550 --> 00:03:17.147
Nothing unusual. People often rely on heuristics rather than calculating perfectly, because full optimisation is impossible.

49
00:03:18.250 --> 00:03:20.447
Isn't a heuristic just laziness?

50
00:03:21.550 --> 00:03:31.551
Not at all. Bounded rationality means that choosing quickly can be wiser than agonising endlessly over every trade-off.

51
00:03:32.650 --> 00:03:34.499
So I should have stopped sooner?

52
00:03:35.583 --> 00:03:46.339
Probably. Setting criteria and picking the first option that meets them, rather than searching for perfection, usually yields better results and less regret.

53
00:03:47.500 --> 00:03:48.931
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:49.783 --> 00:03:52.700
Grammar focus: Rather than plus gerund.

55
00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:54.245
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:54.950 --> 00:03:58.482
People often rely on heuristics rather than calculating perfectly.

57
00:03:59.383 --> 00:04:03.217
She chose to decide quickly rather than agonising over every detail.

58
00:04:04.117 --> 00:04:07.985
Rather than maximising utility, many people settle for good enough.

59
00:04:08.933 --> 00:04:20.212
After 'rather than' keep the same form as the first action: rely on X rather than calculating; decide rather than agonise. With 'would rather' use the bare infinitive: I would rather wait than rush.

60
00:04:20.950 --> 00:04:25.239
Be careful! Don't say: They rely on rules rather than to calculate.

61
00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:30.175
Say: They rely on rules rather than calculating.

62
00:04:30.475 --> 00:04:31.952
rather than + -ing.

63
00:04:32.767 --> 00:04:35.663
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:36.467 --> 00:04:38.130
Choosing Under Limits.

65
00:04:38.430 --> 00:04:46.629
Classical decision theory describes a perfectly rational agent who calculates the expected utility of every option and selects the best.

66
00:04:47.633 --> 00:04:54.881
Real people, however, face uncertainty, limited time and finite attention, which Herbert Simon called bounded rationality.

67
00:04:55.867 --> 00:05:01.930
Rather than maximising, most of us satisfice: we search until an option is good enough and then stop.

68
00:05:02.933 --> 00:05:11.504
Heuristics, though sometimes producing systematic errors, are often remarkably efficient, and they spare us from the paralysis of endless comparison.

69
00:05:12.500 --> 00:05:18.598
Risk aversion adds another twist, since many prefer a sure gain to a gamble with higher expected value.

70
00:05:19.700 --> 00:05:23.806
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:24.650 --> 00:05:27.845
People rely on heuristics rather than blank perfectly.

72
00:05:28.145 --> 00:05:32.066
A, calculate. B, calculating. C, to calculate.

73
00:05:35.600 --> 00:05:37.994
The answer is B: calculating.

74
00:05:39.150 --> 00:05:42.020
A mental shortcut for quick judgment is a blank.

75
00:05:42.320 --> 00:05:46.589
A, heuristic. B, trade-off. C, payoff.

76
00:05:50.133 --> 00:05:52.237
The answer is A: heuristic.

77
00:05:53.383 --> 00:05:57.391
Limited time and information restrict our reasoning: this is blank.

78
00:05:57.691 --> 00:06:02.401
A, risk aversion. B, bounded rationality. C, optimal choice.

79
00:06:05.967 --> 00:06:08.814
The answer is B: bounded rationality.

80
00:06:09.950 --> 00:06:11.729
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:06:12.029 --> 00:06:18.237
A, She waited rather than rushed.. B, She waited rather than rushing.. C, She waited rather than to rush..

82
00:06:21.800 --> 00:06:24.740
The answer is B: She waited rather than rushing..

83
00:06:25.883 --> 00:06:28.417
According to the reading, 'satisficing' means…

84
00:06:28.717 --> 00:06:35.250
A, maximising every time. B, stopping at good enough. C, avoiding all choices.

85
00:06:38.800 --> 00:06:41.078
The answer is B: stopping at good enough.

86
00:06:42.267 --> 00:06:45.302
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:46.150 --> 00:06:50.530
Why might someone rely on a heuristic instead of calculating the 'optimal' choice?

88
00:07:07.317 --> 00:07:08.724
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:07:09.024 --> 00:07:39.708
Someone might rely on a heuristic because calculating the optimal choice costs time, effort and information that they may not have. Rather than searching exhaustively, they use a shortcut that is usually good enough. In a fast-changing situation, a quick decision can be better than a perfect one that arrives too late. The danger is systematic bias, so heuristics should be checked when stakes are high.

90
00:07:40.008 --> 00:07:41.671
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:42.583 --> 00:07:46.745
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and rather than plus gerund.

92
00:07:47.045 --> 00:07:51.625
Today's value is rational reflection. Pause to think carefully before you decide.

93
00:07:51.925 --> 00:07:54.751
See you in Unit sixteen. Goodbye!

94
00:07:55.051 --> 00:07:58.969
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

